WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:09.610 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  growing up home alone was one of my  favorite films there was just something  so devilishly satisfying about watching  a kid my own age take on two completely  incompetent crooks I mean come on but as  I sit here 27 years later I can't help  but wonder what made this film so great  was it Macaulay Culkin over-the-top gags  or was it something deeper something  buried within the structure of the film  itself in this essay I want to explore  how the hero's journey shapes the  narrative of home alone giving us not  only an emotionally satisfying film but  one that solidifies itself as an  all-time holiday classic home alone came  out in 1990 written and produced by John  Hughes yes the John Hughes who wrote and  directed such classics as Ferris  Bueller's Day Off The Breakfast Club and  Uncle Buck in fact it was Hughes who was  so taken aback by Culkin's work that he  recommended him for the lead role of  Kevin McCallister to director Chris  Columbus what do you do for a living  lots of things where's your office I  don't have one okay I don't need one  where's your wife don't have one how  come it's a long story  so we already have this incredible team  in place and yes incredible teams do  wonders for films but they are nothing  without a great story and John Hughes is  no stranger to great storytelling in  most of his films we get a structure  known as the hero's journey  Sixteen Candles Ferris Bueller's Day Off  and even The Breakfast Club follow it so  what exactly is it I've identified three  different iterations that have gained  traction within the world of  screenwriting first is Joseph Campbell's  1949 magnum opus the hero with a  thousand faces the funny thing about  this one is that it's not even a book  about screenwriting in it Joseph  Campbell and American mythologist writer  and lecturer found distinct  commonalities between Christian and  European tales and those of pre-colonial  Native America how could two cultures  completely separated from one another  have stories that followed identical  structures his theory then was based on  the observation that a common pattern  exists  beneath the narrative elements of most  great myths regardless of their origin  or time of creation in his video essay  every story is the same will shoulder  puts it like this indeed after years and  years of studying Campbell concluded  there are characteristics of an  effective story and those  characteristics are consistent  regardless of religion race time or  ancestry armed with this information  Campbell went on to create the monomyth  a 17 step journey a hero must take an  order to create a satisfying narrative  now if it looks daunting at first that's  because it is as Dan Harmon described  the hero the Thousand Faces is very  dense  it's very academic prose you know and  I'm a bad reader in the first place so  in the early 90s Christopher Vogler a  writer who had worked for companies such  as Disney Fox and Warner Brothers  simplified this idea into 12 steps and  applied to the craft of screenwriting  and many films very successful ones I  might add follow this formula including  Harry Potter  The Matrix The Lion King and lore of the  Rings if you're interested in exploring  Vogler's 12 steps i've included a link  to sarah McGuire's breakdown of all  those films in the description but for  Harmon it still wasn't simple enough and  I'm also just obsessed with like  simplicity like I don't believe that I  think everything has to be kind of  symmetrical and simple as simple if it's  actually true so harmon decided to  condense Campbell's 17 steps into eight  fitting it into what he called a story  circle this is the same methodology he  applied to shows he created like  community and Rick and Morty and this is  what I want to use today to examine the  journey of Kevin McCallister and home  alone so I'm going to go step by step  explaining it as we go the first step  Harmon describes is you establish a  protagonist this is you guessed it and  where you establish your protagonist or  hero in their natural setting for Kevin  that's his home the night before his  family's big trip but more importantly  his natural setting is one in which he  is repeatedly ignored by his family seen  as a nuisance don't you know how to  knock phlegm wad can't do anything for  himself  you're completely help  everyone has to do everything and looked  on as a troublesome brat little jerk  this is all established in the first 10  minutes culminating in this  heartbreaking final interaction between  Kevin and his mother my whole life and I  don't want to see anybody else either I  hope you don't mean that you'd feel  pretty sad if you woke up tomorrow  morning and you didn't have a family  no step to need something ain't quite  right this is where we learned  something's off in the world that things  could be better  a storm knocks out the power triggering  a sequence of events culminating and  Kevin being left home alone I've made my  family disappear  Kevin's need whether he knows it or not  is to realize the importance of families  something his journey will help them  figure out which leads to step 3 go or  crossing the threshold this is what your  story is about this is where your  character descends from the known world  to the unknown  Dan Harmon describes this as your movie  poster  perhaps your story is about a killer  shark the Mafia or even a kid home alone  in step 3 we see Kevin enjoying his  newfound freedom by eating endless  amounts of ice cream watching violent  movies and ordering pizza but he soon  realizes it's all a facade he can't  possibly survive on his own so we must  venture out into the world step 4 search  the road of trials this is the meat and  potatoes of the film Christopher Vogler  calls this phase friends enemies and  allies while Harmon prefers to use  Campbell's original designation the road  of trials this the sink-or-swim portion  of the film where our hero must learn to  adapt or flounder as consequence and  Kevin is good at adapting the kid who  once couldn't do anything for himself  finds himself buying groceries stealing  a toothbrush tricking the burglars that  someone is home with an elaborate cutout  display and not to mention overcoming  his fear of the furnace the visual  representation of him getting over his  fear of the unknown  step 5 find meeting the goddess this is  where our hero finds what he's been  looking for whether it's something he  realized or not it is where the need  from step 2 is fulfilled for Kevin the  road of trials has led him to the  realization that his want of being home  alone is nothing compared to his need of  being with family now the meeting with  the goddess doesn't have to be an actual  goddess it can be a person object idea  or revelation these events lead us to  step 6  take meet your maker this is where our  hero takes control of his destiny now  armed with the knowledge of his journey  he rigs his home like a giant game of  mouse traps seemingly out winning the  burglars at every turn but this is also  where what Harmon calls meet your maker  happens it is where the price of your  journey takes its toll kevin has finally  foiled by the burglars and it looks like  all is lost but this price comes with a  goal achieved that we never knew we had  Campbell calls this the rescue from  without and it is the seventh stage of  Harmons story circle the return in Star  Wars this is where Hawn fires at Vader's  spinning his TIE fighter out of control  to give Luke enough time to finish the  mission home alone this actually happens  twice once where Kevin is saved by  buzzes tarantula and another when old  man Marley puts a shovel to the burglars  heads  with the burglars now in the loving care  of the Chicago PD Cowan has finally  returned to some semblance of normalcy  but not quite which leads to the final  and arguably most important step 8  change master of both worlds Kevin once  unable to do anything for himself hated  by his family and equally not wanting  them in his life has gone through this  journey he has changed he's earned the  respect of his family but most  importantly learned the importance of  family  so to recap you see a character in his  natural setting he needs something and  only through the journey of going  searching finding and taking does he  return a changed man I think part of  what makes the hero's journey and story  circle so compelling is that it makes  you think about character in ways you  may otherwise never have thought is it  the only way to structure a film  certainly not but it forces you to look  at both the internal and external  desires of your character something many  films don't do it forces you to see how  they change not mechanically but  organically this is a part of the reason  I think a movie like home alone still  stands up after almost 30 years we live  for the journey to see our protagonists  descend into the unknown and emerge a  hero so this holiday season when the  matrix Star Wars jaws or even home alone  comes on the television break it down  and see the commonalities that connect  us all in this crazy notion of that  which we call story oh and one more  thing hey if you liked what you saw make  sure to LIKE subscribe and check out the  other videos below  [Music]

